From d885f1614b2fb9af50c023e6d1154efdc9eb5f81 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Erik Conijn <egc112@msn.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 12:51:21 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] ARM: dts: BCM5301X: EA6900, R6400: set WAN MAC from nvram

The WAN port's MAC address is et0macaddr + 1, which user space sets on
the port today. Read it from nvram instead, as 382 does for the
RT-N18U. Both device trees already have the et0macaddr cell for gmac2;
the R6400 v2 includes the R6400 v1 device tree and gets it too.

The port then has an address of its own from the start, so it keeps
it when it moves to the other CPU port; a port without one takes the
address of its new conduit, which on these boards is the LAN address,
and 703 refuses that move.

The value is the one OpenWrt sets from user space when nvram has no
wan_hwaddr; the R6400 v2 holds the same value in wan_hwaddr.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Erik Conijn <egc112@msn.com>
---
--- a/arch/arm/boot/dts/broadcom/bcm4708-linksys-ea6900.dts
+++ b/arch/arm/boot/dts/broadcom/bcm4708-linksys-ea6900.dts
@@ -120,6 +120,9 @@
 	ports {
 		port@0 {
 			label = "wan";
+
+			nvmem-cells = <&et0macaddr 1>;
+			nvmem-cell-names = "mac-address";
 		};
 
 		port@1 {
--- a/arch/arm/boot/dts/broadcom/bcm4708-netgear-r6400-v1.dts
+++ b/arch/arm/boot/dts/broadcom/bcm4708-netgear-r6400-v1.dts
@@ -175,6 +175,9 @@
 	ports {
 		port@0 {
 			label = "wan";
+
+			nvmem-cells = <&et0macaddr 1>;
+			nvmem-cell-names = "mac-address";
 		};
 
 		port@1 {
